## Guiding principle
Keep existing LabVIEW architecture largely intact; only change what is necessary for a clean push paradigm. Task should stay simple for the animal but structured enough that behavioural and neural events are interpretable.

## Locked trial structure (v1)
```text
forepaw on rest pad
  ↓
trial starts
  ↓
reach to object
  ↓
push object forward
  ↓
(perturbation / axial resistance)
  ↓
success distance reached (and held in target band)
  ↓
success cue (auditory, immediate)
  ↓
short fixed delay
  ↓
lick spout extends
  ↓
water reward → spout retracts
```

Clean behavioural epochs for neural alignment:
`rest → movement initiation → object contact → push → perturbation → success cue → post-success delay → spout/lick/reward`

Without the rest pad, the animal could keep the paw on the object between trials (pre-load, continuous tactile feedback) so trial onset would not be a clean event. Rest pad does **not** claim identical posture every trial — it defines a **pre-contact trial start state**. Precedent: Estebanez et al., Voigt et al., Galiñanes et al., Mangin et al. (rest/hold before reach).

Delayed retractable spout separates push completion from licking/reward so those don’t collapse into the same window as movement/perturbation (Guo et al. motorized lickport + delay; Wagner et al. ~1 s delay on robotic manipulandum; licking can dominate cortex — Kondo & Matsuzaki, Murphy et al.; delayed water waiting is learnable — Gao et al.). Training: pair the buzzer with water from the first head-fix session, keep that interval short while the push is being discovered, then lengthen cue-to-spout only after the movement is reliable so the cue remains a discrete CS.

## Behavioural / session design
- 1D forelimb **object-push**: reward only if object **ends and remains** in a target distance band (Case 3 `end` ROI + `end TO` — not merely past a minimum). Overshoot past the far edge is **not** an immediate abort: Case 3 already keeps the trial open until `move time`, so the mouse can release/correct back into the zone. Leaving the band only resets the dwell timer. Immediate-fail-on-overshoot would fight the later SPE/perturbation design.
- Session blocks via parameter file:
  1. Baseline (`mag = 0`)
  2. Random perturbations (unpredictable axial resistance)
  3. Fixed perturbations (learnable axial resistance)
  4. Washout (`mag = 0`) — overshoot / velocity / corrective sub-movements as internal-model readouts
- Perturbation = **axial resistance along push axis** (not lateral force as in original Mathis task)

## Locked training progression (revised 07/09/26; supersedes 14/08/26 threshold-crossing-heavy plan)
Criterion-based, ~3–6 weeks. Teach one component at a time, but chain rest-pad contact into pushing
early. Keep `mag = 0` until the final unperturbed task is stable. Advance after the criterion on
**two consecutive days**, not one lucky session. Change only one important parameter at a time.
Formal table starts at head-fix (no separate “handling / reward familiarisation” stage — Mathis also
did not report one). Handling still happens off-protocol.

Revised stages:
1. **Head-fix habituation + cue–reward association** — ramp ~5 → 10 → 15–20 → 25–30 min; spout
   available; buzzer then free water. Do **not** install the final 500–1000 ms delay on day 1 of
   head-fix if the next stage will reward immediately; keep the cue–reward interval short (0–250 ms)
   until the push is solid, then lengthen in stage 6. Stop free pairings once voluntary pushing starts.
2. **Micro-push discovery** — 20–50 genuine designated-paw forward pushes (~0.5–1 mm, above noise);
   ~1–2 sessions max; no zone/hold. Do not reward rest-pad contact alone, nose/wrong-paw/body hits,
   grasp/pull, or spring-return motion.
3. **Proximal broad target zone** — immediately after discovery. Broad ~3–4 mm zone near the paw,
   inside-zone + ~30–50 ms dwell (dwell already anti-ballistic: 4 mm / 50 ms ⇒ must be slower than
   ~80 mm/s through the zone). Correction allowed until the movement-window timeout.
4. **Progressive zone translation** — shift the same broad zone farther forward (~0.5–1 mm after a
   strong block, not after every trial) to the final ~6–7 mm distance (confirm after measuring
   comfortable ROM with the printed paddle).
5. **Final target-zone refinement** — narrow to ~2–3 mm; dwell 50 ms, 100 ms only if needed.
6. **Spout retraction + final reward sequence** — cue stays at target acquisition; then 0 → 250 →
   500 → possibly 1000 ms cue-to-spout; do not change distance/zone in the same sessions.
7. **Expert stabilisation** — freeze geometry and timing; ≥80% success, ≥90% designated paw, little
   first-to-last-third decline, enough trials for the future session.
8. **Zero-force shadow sessions** — exact 50 / 75 / 100 / 75 block labels with magnet commands
   running at force 0. Tests fatigue, satiety, magnet noise/heating, 300-trial feasibility.
9. **Resistance pilot (separate mice)** then **main experiment**. Do not “calibrate” force on the
   imaging cohort. Random-block definition still TBD (presence vs magnitude vs both). Project already
   requires a proportional driver because random sessions are intended to vary force magnitude.

Optional `Rest only` remains a **fallback** for mice that never touch the pad (50–100 ms contact,
release-to-rearm, leave after ~30 contacts or 1–2 sessions). Not a default stage.

Mathis 2017 closest precedent: random water while first head-fixed ≤15 min → 3–5 days any
touch/pull → >1 s start-box + ~3 mm broad box / 100 ms hold → after >60% for 2 days, ~7 mm target
→ train to >80%, typically 4–5 weeks. Wagner 2020 for daily manipulandum troubleshooting and
animal-specific geometry. Roth lever-push and Linares-García 2025 for “push is less natural than
pull.” Mosberger: early reinforcement biases strategy, so do not linger on ballistic threshold
crossing.

### Parameter-file column map
User confirmed 29 Sep 2026: the **27-column** files have the correct order.
Follow `parameters/README.md`. White/push goes in the columns named X
(indices 0, 2, 10, 12). Red/lateral goes in the columns named Y. The loader
reads cases 0–26, including reward delay, cue, and spout. There is no ×2 on
`home X2`.

The table below is the 08/09/26 21-column map. Its X/Y axis labels are
reversed relative to the live loader. Do not write a new file from it.

| Index | Field | Axis / role |
|------|--------|----------------|
| 0, 2 | `home X1`, `home X2` | OBSOLETE LABEL: this row called X lateral. Live files put **push** here. |
| 1, 3 | `home Y1`, `home Y2` | OBSOLETE LABEL: this row called Y push. Live files put **lateral** here. |
| 4 | `home TO` | ms; AND FSR |
| 5–8 | `start` X/Y | unused while start TO = 0 |
| 9 | `start TO` | keep 0 |
| 10, 12 | `end X1`, `end X2` | OBSOLETE LABEL: live files put the **push** target here. |
| 11, 13 | `end Y1`, `end Y2` | OBSOLETE LABEL: live files put **lateral** here. |
| 14 | `end TO` | ms dwell |
| 15, 16 | `mag`, `magtime` | 0 / 25 until resistance |
| 17 | `watertime` | 130/185/240 → 4/6/8 µl |
| 18 | `timeout` | Case 0 ITI (ms) |
| 19 | `move time` | Case 3 window (ms). The shorter-pull file used **400** |
| 20 | `light` | keep 0 |

Old pull files (typical values): home `2.39 2 2.8 3`, home TO `500`, start TO `0`,
end shaped per filename, end TO `50`, mag `0` or `2.2`, watertime `100`/`200`,
timeout `1000`, move time `400`. **Do not copy those end voltages or `magtime=100`.**

How they actually trained: one **identical-row** file per stage (500–612 lines; one 2964-line
baseline), then swapped files when the mouse was ready. Target “center/bound” lived in the
filename (`2.55center`, `2.3bound`, `.05bound` = ±0.05 V ≈ ±1 mm on the original 0.05 V/mm
assumption). “UNBOXED” files widened the end box so overshoot still counted. Experimental
sessions were concatenated blocks in one file (e.g. Task1 ≈ 157 zero-force + 100 × `mag=2.2` /
`magtime=100`). **Do not copy those voltages or `magtime=100`.** Original perturbation was a
brief lateral kick at trial start, not sustained axial resistance 100 ms after movement onset.
Confirm the live unbundle map on the rig before writing any push file.

### Practical LabVIEW / rig implications from this plan (07/09/26)
Already enough for stages 2–5 once geometry and volts-to-mm exist: `end` box + `end TO` is the
zone+dwell; Case 3 already allows correction until `move time`; `home TO` is the rest-pad hold
(FSR is ANDed into that same timer); `start TO=0` is fine because rest-pad + object-home replaces
Mathis’ start-box wait; `mag=0` throughout training.
- **Need before first mouse, not a new state machine:** shaft+handle+constrainer are fitted and
  the ripped FSR was replaced 08/09/26 with protective tape (working). Water µl/pulse is done
  (`calibration/water-volume.md`; 130/185/240 ms → 4/6/8 µl). Still: spring return without
  bounce into the micro-push threshold, freeze rest-to-object geometry, volts-to-mm, joystick
  noise, actuator settle time, FSR hysteresis on the taped surface, and which logged axis
  actually moves (`Y_POS` index 0 vs `X_POS` index 1).
- **Need for stage 1 (small LabVIEW):** a habituation control that runs `cue → delay → water`
  without a successful push. Manual water exists; the automated pair does not, because the water
  loop only wakes on Case 4.
- **Early-training movement window:** user declined the FSR lift-off Case 5
  edit (09/09/26). Case 3 still does `(now − trail start) > move time` with
  `trail start` written in Case 2. Lengthen column 20 (`move time`) instead;
  bench file is 30000 ms. Do not edit the Case 2 output constant 3.
- **Need before useful training data:** event logging + session metadata (stage, file, every
  front-panel value). Trajectory logging already exists.
- **Nice before animals, not blocking shaping:** FSR hysteresis/debounce; optional paw-release
  before rearm so a planted paw cannot auto-retrigger; session summary of success / wrong-paw
  (wrong-paw is video, not DAQ).
- **Defer until resistance:** movement-onset detector, 100 ms delayed magnet onset, hold `mag`
  until trial end instead of a 100 ms pulse. Do not reuse old `magtime=100`. Velocity-settling
  gate is optional if 50 ms dwell in a 2–4 mm zone already rejects ballistic crossings.
- **Parameter files are 27 columns (09/09/26).** Indices 21–26: `reward delay`,
  `cue duration`, `spout extend`, `spout settle`, `consumption`, `spout retract`.
  Files are updated. Loader 21→27, both FP cluster copies (`exp values` and
  `exp para array`), cases 21–26, and water-loop field wiring were completed
  and reported runtime-working on the rig PC 09/09/26. Keep old FP controls
  temporarily as references/fallbacks. **Repository import DONE 21/09/26:** all
  four rig-tested binaries copied into repo root (`Push Behaviour_MCHALABI.vi`,
  `avg joystick and frame trig lick3.vi`, `PushTask Globals.vi`, `frame counter.vi`);
  SHA-256 verified against Downloads transfer; main VI updated (251539 B, 27-col
  loader + water-loop wiring); three helpers byte-identical to prior repo copies.
  Stage 6 files: `parameters/training/06_delay_{0,250,500,1000}.txt`.

### Training controls to add to the canonical main VI
- Use one main VI with a `Training stage` enum: `Habituation`, `Rest only` (fallback),
  `Micro push`, `Proximal zone`, `Zone translation`, `Zone refine`, `Delay`, `Expert`,
  `Shadow`, `Experiment`. Do not maintain separate stage-specific VIs.
- Adjustable/logged controls: FSR on/off thresholds + hysteresis, rest hold, push threshold, target
  bounds/hold, cue enable/duration, reward delay, spout extend/retract voltages, settle time,
  consumption time.
- Safe manual controls: water, cue test, spout extend/retract, hardware output enable, plus a
  habituation button/mode that runs the full `cue → delay → water` sequence without Case 4.
- Live indicators: task state, FSR voltage/paw state, joystick position, success criterion, cue state,
  commanded spout position.
- Keep hardware calibration values on the VI; use stage-specific parameter files for behavioural
  criteria. Log the selected stage and every adjustable value with each session.

## Current VI architecture (original Mathis code — still the base)
Five-state machine in middle While loop (state in shift register) + async helper loops via occurrences/queues:

| Case | Role |
|------|------|
| 0 | Inter-trial gate: object in `home` ROI held for `home TO` **AND** `timeout` elapsed → Case 1 |
| 1 | Wait for object in `start` ROI held for `start TO` → Case 2 |
| 2 | Stamp trial start; **Set Occurrence** (wakes magnet loop) → Case 3 |
| 3 | Movement window: timeout → fail to Case 0; enter `end` ROI + hold `end TO` → Case 4 |
| 4 | Success: **Set reward occurrence** (wakes water loop); stamp inter-trial clock → Case 0 |

- **Magnet loop:** Wait on Occurrence → AO `Magnets` = `mag` for `magtime`, then 0; increments `Trial #`
- **Water loop:** Wait on Occurrence → DO `water` True for `watertime`, then False (Flat Sequence in occurrence-received / `False` timeout case)
- ROI checks AND X-in-range with Y-in-range; per-trial params from `exp para array` (text file → loader For-loop → cluster array)
- Live rig NI-MAX tasks (04/08/26): `joysticklickframe`, `Magnets_Dev1`, `Water_Dev1`, `frame counter_Dev1`, `MyPulseOutputTask`, `testing1blox_2labviews`
- Confirmed `joysticklickframe`: X=`Dev1/ai0`, Y=`Dev1/ai1`, differential ±10 V, continuous 10 kHz; device is **NI PCIe-6321**

### Already supported (configure via params / minimal code)
1. **Bounded target zone** — Case 3 `end` ROI + `end TO`
2. **Block structure** — one parameter-file line per trial
3. **Washout kinematics** — full trajectory already logged; analyze offline
4. **1D via params + physical constraint** — keep LabVIEW 2D coordinate structure; redefine task-relevant axis in parameter file; **physically constrain lateral axis with the existing 3D-printed 1D-axis constrainer** (no need to delete Y logic for v1)

### Real work remaining
(a) print and fit the joystick shaft plus a chosen handle, then add spring return and the steel
target, (b) finalize the animal-safe FSR cap and hysteresis, (c) calibrate volts-to-mm, joystick
noise, water µl/pulse and spout settle, then write stage parameter files in the existing 21-column
format, (d) add habituation cue–reward triggering, event logging and session metadata, (e) later:
movement-onset-triggered axial resistance and force-calibrate the Ledex, (f) only then Baseline /
Random / Fixed / Washout files.

## Locked hardware & design decisions (Weekly Update 29/06/26)

| Role | Choice | DAQ | Notes |
|------|--------|-----|-------|
| 1D constraint | Existing **3D-printed 1D-axis constrainer** | — | Lateral axis may remain in code; physically constrained |
| Push object | 3D-modeled **89 mm joystick shaft** + interchangeable tip-mounted handle (rounded cube or 38 mm bar) | reuse push-axis AI | Position contact surface **below the mouse** in its natural forelimb workspace and close to the rest pad; use a short forgiving reach and push-biased geometry that discourages grasping like the original pull handles. The cube has three holes to set reach and object weight over the joystick. Animal leaves rest → contacts → pushes (not continuous grip across ITI). Spring-loaded return to home. Shape push distance gradually in training. |
| Rest-pad gate | **Both** required: object in home (spring-loaded) **AND** forepaw on rest pad | — | Binary trial-gating, not posture standardization |
| Rest-pad sensor | **FSR** (binary on/off via threshold + hold duration) | spare **AI** → LabVIEW | Not a precision force meter. **Not** real-time DLC for v1 (slower to stand up); revisit vision trigger only if FSR false pos/neg during piloting |
| Retractable lick spout | **Actuonix linear actuator** | new **AO** position command (0–5 V); separate motor PSU, shared GND | Simple reliable in/out; keep existing `water` DO for valve |
| Success cue | **Brief auditory cue** at trial success | new **DO** (or AO) to clicker/speaker driver | **Supersedes** earlier ERM vibration plan (Mackenzie: vibe adds mass, changes object mechanics, imaging movement artefacts). Cue is discrete, timestamped, separated from perturbation onset and reward |
| Axial resistance | Ledex `195224-230` (EU/CH equivalent of Mathis `69905K25`), mounted **axially** | `Dev1/ao0` (`MagnetPush_Dev1`) | Received 02/09/26; not mounted or wired. Copy mesoscope drive electronics. Force-calibrate; do not assume 2.2 V / 100 ms transfers |

### Superseded (do not implement for v1)
- ERM vibration motor on the manipulandum (Adafruit 1201 + MOSFET 5648) — rejected after Mackenzie discussion
- Real-time DLC as primary rest-pad trigger — deferred fallback only

### Hardware selections and status — picks finalized 09/07/26
- **Rest-pad FSR:** Interlink **FSR 402** solder-tab (PN 30-81794), ~$3 — buyinterlinkelectronics.com / Mouser-Digikey (search 30-81794). Backup: FSR 402 Short Tail (34-00015) if pad is tiny; FSR X 402 for durability. + one **10 kΩ** divider resistor + shielded wire → spare **AI** (channel TBD after live inventory; `ai0` and `ai1` are occupied). `paw_on = V > threshold`.
- **Lick spout:** **Actuonix L12-30-50-12-I** (30 mm, 50:1, 12 V, integrated controller, **0–5 V** position mode). EU: noDNA (~€173) / RS stock 202-0768 (~£146). Ships with brackets + clevis/threaded rod ends. Backup MB1 clevis (Firgelli). Avoid -R/-P (PWM / external LAC). + **12 V ≥1 A PSU, shared GND** + 3D-print carriage + blunt lick tube + tubing. Keep existing `water` DO valve.
- **Auditory cue:** start with **Adafruit 5 V active buzzer (#1536, ~$1)** on a **DO** line (fixed ~2 kHz beep, trivial LabVIEW). Upgrade for chosen frequency: passive piezo (TDK PS1240) or small speaker + **Adafruit PAM8302 amp (#2130)** driven by a **6321 counter output** (LabVIEW-native square wave, ~50–100 ms). Teensy+audio only if calibrated tones later needed.
- **Push object:** CAD imported 05/09/26 — 89 mm shaft plus tip-mounted bar or rounded-cube handle. Print/fit still pending. Add a light return spring (auto-return to home) and reuse the push-axis readout. Ferromagnetic target: Mathis used a small steel ring on an aluminum handle base with **no catalog number**. Make one from a **mild-steel DIN 125 washer** (zinc-plated carbon steel OK; not A2/A4 stainless). Solenoid face is ~20 mm; a washer OD of ~12–20 mm (M6–M10) is a starting size. Embed/glue it on the object facing the solenoid. Mount the pull solenoid on the home side so attraction resists the push.
- **Perturbation magnet (ARRIVED 02/09/26):** Ledex / Johnson Electric **`195224-230`**, STA 75L **60° pull**, 12 VDC 7 W 20.7 Ω, Contact Evolution SA (Payerne). ~20 mm × 40 mm tubular body. Used like Mathis: coil as electromagnet attracting a steel target, not as a plunger mechanically linked to the object. Drive via `MagnetPush_Dev1` on `Dev1/ao0`; do not power the coil from the DAQ AO pin.

### RIG CONSTRAINT — AO channels are the bottleneck
Live rig uses **NI PCIe-6321** (not the previously assumed 6251). Actuonix is installed on `Dev1/ao1`. The received training-rig magnet (Ledex `195224-230`) is intended for `Dev1/ao0` but not yet wired. Both outputs are software-timed/on-demand; NI documents support for one such AO task per physical AO channel. Therefore keep magnet support as a one-channel AO0 task and the spout as an independent one-channel AO1 task. Hardware-timed AO would instead require a combined task.

### Target DAQ channel map (post-additions)
Training rig target: AI X=`Dev1/ai0` + AI Y=`Dev1/ai1` + AI rest pad=`Dev1/ai2` · AO magnet=`Dev1/ao0` (`MagnetPush_Dev1`; Ledex `195224-230` received, not yet wired) · AO lick-spout position=`Dev1/ao1` · DO water=`Dev1/port0/line0` · DO success cue=`Dev1/port0/line1` · frame sync=`Dev1/ctr0` input on `PFI8`; pulse output=`Dev1/ctr1` on `PFI13` at 50 Hz. Mesoscope-rig magnet mapping remains to be audited separately.

