### 4. Auditory success cue + delayed spout reward (Water loop)
Insert into water loop **occurrence-received (`False`) case**, inside the existing Flat Sequence:

- Live audit 18/08/26 confirms the current functional frames are exactly:
  `Water_Dev1 HIGH → wait watertime → Water_Dev1 LOW`. Preserve these and add frames around them.
- Final saved tasks created 18/08/26: `SuccessCue_Dev1`=`Dev1/port0/line1`, digital one-sample
  on-demand; `LickSpout_Dev1`=`Dev1/ao1`, 0–5 V, one-sample on-demand
- Three empty cue frames inserted before the original water-HIGH frame on 18/08/26; existing
  water HIGH/wait/LOW sequence remains intact
- Cue frames wired 18/08/26 as `SuccessCue_Dev1 HIGH → Wait cue duration → SuccessCue_Dev1 LOW`;
  front-panel `cue duration (ms)` control added with default 50 ms. Runtime test passed: one brief
  cue occurred per successful trial and stopped cleanly
- Five empty reward/spout frames inserted 18/08/26 in the correct order: three between cue LOW and
  water HIGH (reward delay, extend, settle), two after water LOW (consumption, retract)
- Reward/spout frames wired 18/08/26 with front-panel defaults: reward delay=0 ms;
  `LickSpout_Dev1` extend initially 1.0 V with auto-start; settle initially 3000 ms;
  consumption=2000 ms; retract=0 V with auto-start. First runtime test produced expected coarse extension but also slight
  continued fine extension during the familiar audible post-arrival settling period. Determine
  whether motion/sound consistently stops within the 3000 ms window before classifying as normal
  closed-loop settling versus persistent hunting
- User-selected bench controls updated 18/08/26: cue=50 ms, reward delay=0 ms, spout extend=3.0 V,
  settle=2000 ms, consumption=2000 ms, retract=0 V, rest-pad threshold=0.01 V. Actuator settling
  sound/fine movement accepted provisionally. Automated sequence moves spout but currently delivers
  no water, while manual water delivery still works. `watertime` confirmed nonzero at 200 ms and no
  solenoid click is audible. NI-MAX `Water_Dev1` False→True→False test produces clicks and water;
  LabVIEW close-up confirms HIGH data=True and LOW data=False. Next inspect Water HIGH DAQmx error;
  Water HIGH error indicator remained clear during a failed automated reward. This proves the HIGH
  write executes and strongly indicates LOW follows immediately because the middle duration frame
  lacks an effective wait. Adding Wait(ms)=200 produced a click but no water. Do not assume placement:
  user reports the Wait was already put in a dedicated frame and the original VI delivers water
  without this added node. Obtain readable close-ups of the modified and original
  `HIGH → timing mechanism → LOW` regions and identify the original blue timing object/wiring before
  further edits
- Diagnostic dedicated 1000 ms HIGH duration succeeded 18/08/26: automated valve produced water.
  Therefore task/channel/write sequencing is functional; 200 ms was below the currently reliable
  effective delivery duration or the line required priming. Retest 200 ms immediately after priming,
  then find and volumetrically calibrate the shortest reliable pulse—do not use 1000 ms for animals
- Retest after the 1000 ms prime confirmed automated 200 ms delivery works. User observed multiple
  air bubbles in the tubing; root cause was an unprimed/air-filled water path, not LabVIEW or DAQ.
  Add tubing inspection + manual prime until bubble-free to every pre-session checklist; retain
  200 ms only as a bench value pending gravimetric µL calibration
- Three spaced full-sequence trials passed 18/08/26: one cue, spout extension, one water delivery,
  and retraction each time, without reported persistent motion or missed rewards. Core v1 hardware
  integration is bench-functional. New explicit 200 ms Wait is nominally equivalent to original
  `watertime=200`, but physical pulse equivalence remains unmeasured; compare delivered volume or
  measure P0.0 HIGH duration before claiming exact equivalence

```text
Frame 0: auditory success cue pulse (immediate on reward occurrence)
Frame 1: Wait reward delay (fixed; introduce gradually in training)
Frame 2: AO lick_spout_position = extend
Frame 3: Wait spout settle
Frame 4–5: water ON / OFF (existing)
Frame 6: optional consumption Wait
Frame 7: AO lick_spout_position = retract
```

Training note: early stages may set delay ≈ 0 and/or deliver cue concurrent with water; later stages lengthen delay so cue precedes spout/reward.

